{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"ea56444e9a9f27ff","type":"text","text":"## Inflation\n\nInflation is a general rise in prices for products and services","x":120,"y":-440,"width":620,"height":160},
		{"id":"452633f249cc60f6","type":"text","text":"### Measuring Inflation\n\nA price index tracks the price of a product or service, or a basket of products and services. Inflation is measured as a % change from one period to another.\n\n### CPI\n\nA consumer price index assumes a basket of goods that reflects a typical households spending and tracks its price over time.\n\n* Weights of the components can be changed over time, like new technologies.\n* Retail price index (RPI) is CPI with housing included\n* Both ignore short term prices volatility\n\n### PPI\n\nMeasure the average selling price of products\n\n* Broader than CPIs, include investment products\n* But do not include services\n* Can be raw materials like oil, metals (b2b)\n\n\n## PPI vs CPI\n\nRelationship between the two can be used to determine how much producers costs are passed on to consumers.\n\n![[Pasted image 20261007093306.png|633]]\n\nr","x":-605,"y":-1040,"width":850,"height":560},
		{"id":"d0413c301c9c329b","type":"text","text":"## Effects of Inflation\n\nConsumers and businesses might change their timing of purchases based on inflation. Spending, saving and borrowing decisions.\n\n### Consumers\n\n* If inflation expected, might buy now rather than save.\n* Choose to borrow as can repay loans with money worth less.\n* Shifts demand from future to present.\n\n## Businesses\n\n* Budgeting because more difficult due to uncertainty of rising prices\n* Generally bad\n* Consumers spend rather than invest so less access to capital for companies.\n* Profits may decline if companies unable to pass higher costs to consumers\n\n### Investments\n\n* Fixed cash amount investments lose value - inflation leads to increased interest rates.\n* Shares may hedge against inflation if selling prices of company increase with input prices.\n\n\n","x":-605,"y":-240,"width":850,"height":320},
		{"id":"2eb0a977d85fb977","type":"text","text":"## Other changes in prices\n\n### Deflation\n\n* Persistent and significant decreases in prices.\n* Consumers will save expecting prices to fall.\n\t* Demand drops, companies reduce production and labour - unemployment.\n\n### Stagflation\n\n* Inflation during low or no economic growth.\n* Often associated with inflation that originates outside country like oil prices.\n* Investment spending by businesses declines.\n* Consumer spending decreases due to prices increasing.\n* Unemployment increases.\n\n### Hyperinflation\n\n* Extreme inflation that causes consumers to struggle to afford many products and services.\n* Associated with emerging markets\n","x":680,"y":-1120,"width":800,"height":640}
	],
